====================================================================================================
5. #683: how much denser the air at a top gets once the column reads the real
   gravity fall-off; and what a layered tail does to Earth's edge
====================================================================================================
  An isothermal column under g(z) = g R^2/(R+z)^2 integrates to
  p = p0 exp[-(R/H) z/(R+z)], so the flat model's density at altitude z is
  light by exp[(z/H) z/(R+z)] -- H at the datum, z just under the top. Small
  for a big world, enormous for a small one.
  sys    body         R km   H_eff m   top km        gain
  solar  Jupiter     69911     27000      270       1.04x
  solar  Uranus      25362     20000      200       1.08x
  solar  Neptune     24622     20000      200       1.08x
  solar  Saturn      58232     60000      600       1.11x
  solar  Earth        6371      8423       85       1.14x
  solar  Venus        6052     16000      160       1.29x
  ksp_s  Jool         6000     20000      200       1.38x
  solar  Mars         3390     11500      115       1.39x
  solar  Titan        2575     20000      200       2.06x
  ksp_s  Laythe        500      5500       50       2.29x
  ksp_s  Shay          550      6000       60       2.67x
  ksp_s  Kerbin        600      5500       70       3.78x
  ksp_s  Eve           700      7000       90       4.33x
  ksp_s  Duna          320      4000       50       5.41x

  Earth's authored 7-level table vs an exponential with the SAME datum and
  the SAME 85 km top, at 84,999 m:
    layered   p     0.3573 Pa  rho  6.669e-06
    exp H 8423 p      4.199 Pa  rho  5.076e-05   -> layered is 11.8x thinner in p, 7.6x in rho

  And the Kerbin option C warning: Earth's table copied VERBATIM onto Kerbin
  (height 70 km) is refused at load -- atmo.h:131 needs L.back().base_alt < top,
  and Earth's last level is based at 71 km. C above is Earth's first SIX levels;
  the 71 km row never governs below 70 km, so dropping it changes nothing.

shellEdge law (bodylimits.h): max(100 km, 1.2 x top) -> 100 km for a 70 km top
